# M25 — posture rule E2 (anon and service_role hold no USAGE on schema app) made
# unmatchable.
#
# Rule E2 is the measured reason exposing a schema is safe at all. With schema `app` served
# by PostgREST, an anonymous request is refused at the SCHEMA boundary — observed end to
# end as `HTTP 401 {"code":"42501","message":"permission denied for schema app"}` — before
# any policy and before any function body. That is grant-absence, which canon 5.2 calls
# strictly stronger than RLS. A single `grant usage on schema app to anon` would move the
# refusal from "cannot enter" to "whatever each function happens to check".
#
# THE REPLACEMENT IS `authenticator`, A ROLE THAT EXISTS AND HOLDS NOTHING. MEASURED:
# has_schema_privilege('authenticator','app','USAGE') = false. This is the M5 trick done
# deliberately — M6 failed because its placeholder role did not exist and the predicate
# raised, whereas a real role with no privilege makes the loop find nothing and the rule go
# quiet, which is what "unmatchable" is supposed to mean.
id: M25
title: posture rule E2 (no client-role USAGE on schema app) made unmatchable
expect: caught
proves: rule E2 reports USAGE on an exposed schema being granted to a browser-reachable role
expect-test: [NFR-02] E2 fires when anon is granted USAGE on schema app
expect-test: [NFR-02] E2 fires when service_role is granted USAGE on schema app

file: supabase/migrations/20261022160000_expose_app_schema_to_postgrest.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
      from unnest(array['anon','service_role']) ro(rolname)
@@REPLACE
      from unnest(array['authenticator']) ro(rolname)
@@END
